  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SAFAL for Integrated Water Resource Management project (SAFAL for IWRM)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">90,000 farming households covering 450,000 men, women and youth optimize water efficient climate-smart and regenerative agricultural production for improving nature-based agri-food system.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SAFAL for IWRM is community-based water management project, enhancing water use efficiency in agriculture to contribute towards a systemic change in the operationalization of IWRM in Southwest Bangladesh. It is promoting community-based sustainable use, protection and restoration of watersheds for resilient agriculture. It targets to deliver 03 inter-related outcomes: 1) improved water and soil efficiency, 2) sustainable harnessing of ground and surface water, and 3) improved local governance of water for agriculture at the micro-water shed level. The project defined 03 pathways to achieve the results related to water resource management, climate change adaptation and agricultural productivity: 1) water-efficient, climate-smart and regenerative agriculture production systems; 2) watershed protection; and 3) Micro-watershed management and governance by local communities. It proposes to implement the project in 80 clusters in 05 districts, reach 90000 farming HH, cover 0.5 ml people.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The overall objective of the SAFAL for IWRM is to develop a community-based water management model for harnessing sustainable use of surface water for agriculture and business innovations for local food system development. Through the community-based water management, SAFAL for IWRM is enhancing ecosystem-based farming and bringing innovations in land-use planning and climate smart and regenerative agriculture. 41,000 hectares of land have Through improved water use efficiency and soil organic matters, 41,000 hectares of land would optimize improved cropping intensity and productivity to supply quality food to the local and national market. A policy paper would be developed to promote sustainable use, protection, and restoration of watersheds for resilient agriculture in Southwest Bangladesh.</narrative>
  </description>
